{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Benjamin, a wolf.</b> Saul — whose kingship was not prolonged; he is likened to a wolf that tears prey, which does not linger over its prey. But the kingship of the house of David, whose kingship was prolonged, is likened to a lion, which does not flee but stands over its prey." "<b>A wolf tears.</b> It tears so many cattle that each morning its eating suffices it until evening, when it distributes the spoils of its prey to its cubs — and it is a parable. The vav of <b>and at evening</b> is superfluous, and there are many like it. And one should not object on account of the zaqef under <b>until</b>, as I explained in perashat VaYigash regarding ״במיטב הארץ״." "<b>And at evening he divides spoil.</b> For such is the habit of the wolf, as it is written: ״זאבי ערב לא גרמו לבקר״ (wolves of the evening did not leave [anything] for the morning)."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:09:19.503730642Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}